infographic compare-swot
data
  title User-defined type guards: power you assert, soundness you owe
  desc The checker trusts `x is T` without proof — every strength rides on an unsound contract
  compares
    - label Strengths
      children
        - label Reusable narrowing
          desc A helper joins the same control-flow analysis as typeof and instanceof
        - label Both branches narrow
          desc true branch becomes T, false branch subtracts T — like a built-in guard
        - label Structural `in` checks
          desc "data" in r narrows union members that share no literal tag
    - label Weaknesses
      children
        - label Unsound by design
          desc The checker never verifies the body proves T — `is T` is taken on faith
        - label `in` is fragile
          desc If both members can carry the key, the test stops distinguishing them
        - label Honest array check is O(n)
          desc every over the whole array is real CPU on huge inputs
    - label Opportunities
      children
        - label Derive from a schema
          desc Zod safeParse or io-ts keep validator and type from drifting apart
        - label Parse once at the boundary
          desc Validate on entry instead of re-guarding the same value everywhere
        - label Prefer a discriminant tag
          desc An explicit kind field beats `in` when you own the shapes
    - label Threats
      children
        - label A lying guard compiles
          desc A body checking less than it claims raises no error at compile time
        - label Silent mass mis-typing
          desc One bad guard narrows thousands of call sites to T
        - label Crash far from the cause
          desc Surfaces as a runtime TypeError nowhere near the guard
theme
  palette #10b981 #f59e0b #6366f1 #f43f5e
